Method: read-only audit. No source, documentation, test, tooling or CI file was modified.
The only artifact written is this report.

## 0. Baseline statement — what v0.0.20 actually is

`src/phonebridge/` contains a domain contract only: package docstrings, Pydantic models,
constant tables, and two abstract kernel interfaces (`LineManager`, `LineAdminKernel`) whose
methods are `...` bodies plus normative docstrings. There is no concrete `InMemoryLineManager`,
no adapter, no SQLite store, no LineProvider, no MediaRouter, no hci-agent, no deployment code —
the roadmap deliberately defers these to milestones M1–M5. `tests/test_smoke.py` is a
two-line `assert True` placeholder. The 80 non-e2e unit tests exercise model validation,
enum/table parity and documentation parsing only.

Consequence for severity ranking: no finding can be a *runtime* defect — everything below is
either (a) a contract/model defect that would be inherited by the M1 implementation,
(b) a contract-vs-spec/doc drift, or (c) a tooling/infra issue. There are no Blockers: the
Phase-0 contract freeze is internally consistent enough to proceed, with the two Major items
closed first.

Verification executed: `pytest -x -q -m "not e2e" --timeout=60` → 80 passed, 1 deselected;
`ruff check` + `ruff format --check` → clean; `mypy -p phonebridge` → clean;
`tools/check_spec_anchors.sh` → pass; `tools/check_version_tag.sh` → pass
(`v0.0.20` is an ancestor of HEAD); `tools/check_changes_entry.sh` → pass.
Two candidate defects were additionally verified by constructing the models in a live
interpreter (see M1 and m1).

## 1. Findings

### MAJOR-1 — `EventEnvelope` validates `(name → audience.scope)` but never `(name → payload type)`: the review-15 item-31 PII/broadcast guard is bypassable at runtime.

Evidence: `src/phonebridge/domain/contract.py:2389-2416` (`EventEnvelope` +
`_audience_scope_legal_for_event`), `contract.py:2342-2386` (`EVENT_AUDIENCE_SCOPES`).

The scope table is keyed on `EventName`; the payload binds to the name only through the
`Generic[PayloadT]` annotation, which Python does not enforce at runtime. Verified live:

```python
EventEnvelope(
    name=EventName.LINE_STATE_CHANGED,
    audience=BroadcastAudience(),
    payload=IncomingCallEvent(call_token="tok-secret", caller_id="+37377777111", ...),
)  # constructs without error
```

The result is a broadcast envelope carrying a one-shot `call_token` and caller PII — exactly
the accident class the docstring claims is now unconstructible ("a PII-bearing event must
never be constructible with BroadcastAudience", contract.py:2408-2409; api-contract.md:241-247).
Any kernel bug that stamps the wrong `name` silently widens a scoped payload; the static
generic is the only guard and mypy does not see kernel-internal dynamic construction.
Fix direction: a model validator asserting `type(payload)` against a name→payload-class
table (e.g. `EVENT_PAYLOAD_MODELS`), or making `payload` a discriminated union keyed by
`name`. This is a contract-model defect that M1 would inherit.

### MAJOR-2 — the 8-hop transitive `forward_via_line` cycle check is normative in three documents but absent from the contract surface.

Required by: `docs/specs/line-manager.md:158-168` (explicit Requirement),
`docs/api-contract.md:519-525` ("a transitively cyclic forward chain (`A → B → … → A`) is
detected by walking the stored policies — bounded at 8 hops — and rejected INVALID_ARGUMENT"),
`docs/phone-bridge-service-design.md:703-705`.

Present in `contract.py`: only the *self-loop* ban — the `LineAssignment` model validator at
contract.py:1660-1664 and the `configure_line` docstring at contract.py:4163-4184, which
enumerates fallback-policy validation at length (E.164 pair, target-line allowlist, self-loop)
without mentioning the transitive walk. No other contract text carries the requirement.

Consequence: an implementer coding M1 from `contract.py` docstrings alone — the file the
freeze designates as the implementer-facing truth — ships `configure_line` without cycle
detection, and `A → B → A` forwarding loops become constructible through the admin surface.
Drift class: normative requirement silently dropped on the contract surface.

### MINOR-1 — `ProviderCallStateUpdate` accepts `reason` on non-disconnected states.

`contract.py:2959-2961` declares `reason: ProviderDisconnectReason | None` with no scoping
validator, while the egress model `CallStateChangedEvent` enforces reason-only-on-
`"disconnected"` (`contract.py:2466-2482`), and the wire document scopes the vocabulary to
`disconnected` updates (`docs/api-contract.md:190`). Verified live:
`state="active", reason=ProviderDisconnectReason.NORMAL` constructs. Ingress laxity only —
the kernel can still ignore the field — but the same scoping validator belongs here for
symmetry.

### MINOR-2 — `ProviderRegistration.profile` / `ProviderCapabilityUpdate.profile` are unbounded external strings.

`contract.py:2830` and `contract.py:3021`: `profile: str | None = None` with no length cap.
`meta` on the same registration model was explicitly bounded under CODING_HAZARDS rule 7
(`contract.py:2831-2874`), but `profile` — also provider-controlled wire input — was not:
a 10 000-char value validates. A cap consistent with `MAX_PROVIDER_META_*` belongs here.

### MINOR-3 — `ErrorData.free_lines` / `AcquireRefused.free_lines` skip the `_check_id` hygiene applied to every other line-id field.

`contract.py:1049` and `contract.py:1907` use bare `tuple[LineId, ...]`; all other line-id
positions use `_CheckedLineId`. Output-only, kernel-produced data, so impact is consistency
rather than exposure — but a `line_id:"default"` alias or a colon-bearing id would pass
validation on these two fields while being rejected everywhere else.

### MINOR-4 — `send_dtmf` docstring lags the wire ALERTING allowance.

`contract.py:3453-3460` states "INVALID_STATE when the lease has no ACTIVE call".
`docs/api-contract.md:217` documents INVALID_STATE *unless* the lease is ACTIVE-with-
`audio_ready` **or ALERTING** (early-media IVR, round 25 — the 0.3–0.6 s
`State=active`→SCO-up gap would otherwise swallow tones). An implementer following the
docstring would falsely gate ALERTING-phase DTMF.

### MINOR-5 — `acquire` docstring omits the `reissued_ticket_id` delivery channel.

`contract.py:3297-3301` says a preempted `enqueue=True` immediate grant's session "learns
its `ticket_id` inside the `event.queue_updated` notification". The authoritative delivery
channel is `event.call_state_changed.reissued_ticket_id` (`contract.py:2450-2457`,
`docs/api-contract.md:237`) — the queue_updated is only the confirmation. Docstring lag on
a round-23 item.

### MINOR-6 — media-credential reissue has no supersede semantics: stale-token replay window.

`call.reattach_media` (`contract.py:3464-3476`), `ProviderReattachMediaCommand`
(`contract.py:2173-2187`) and `register_provider` reissue (`contract.py:3550-3557`) mint a
fresh `(media_endpoint, media_token)` per live lease, but nothing requires the
MediaRouter/MediaAllocator to invalidate the *previous* credential for the same
`(lease_id, role)`. The docstring claim "the burned `provider_media_token` … can never
authenticate" holds only when the old token was actually consumed: a token that was minted
but never delivered (a `line.dial` that died with the socket) or never used (media socket
dropped before `media.auth`) remains valid for the full 30 s `MEDIA_PAIR_TTL_SEC` alongside
its reissued successor — two live credentials for one role, one leakable. Fix direction:
state that reissue atomically supersedes the prior pair entry for the role.

### MINOR-7 — commands drained against a dead provider binding are unspecified (reregister × in-flight `line.dial` race).

After `provider.reregister` mints a fresh binding `assignment_id`, any outbox command still
undelivered on the dropped channel (`line.dial`, `line.answer`, `line.reattach_media`)
addresses the superseded binding. The contract does not state whether the shared
provider-command executor drops such commands or treats them as transport errors. If dropped
silently, a lease in `DIALING` wedges: saga compensation (`contract.py:3397-3407`) fires only
on an adapter-observed `{accepted:false}`/timeout/transport error, and "command never
sendable because its binding died" is not spelled out as one. Fix direction: declare that the
executor feeds `ingest_call_state(DISCONNECTED, NETWORK_ERROR)` for commands whose target
binding no longer exists, matching the `ProviderLiveness` superseded-id drop rule at
`contract.py:2888-2897`.

