## 3. Over-engineering assessment (explicit)

- **Parallel registries.** `EventName` (14 members) ↔ `EVENT_WIRE_NAMES` ↔ `EVENT_AUDIENCE_SCOPES`
  ↔ per-payload `Literal` fields, and again `ProviderCommand` discriminators ↔
  `PROVIDER_COMMAND_WIRE_NAMES`. Four tables that must move together on every new event —
  MAJOR-1 shows they are not cross-validated (name↔payload is the missing leg). The drift
  tests cover enum↔wire parity but not payload↔name. This is the one place where structure
  has already outrun enforcement.
- **Dual timeout models.** `TimeoutConfig` (patch) + `LineTimeouts` (effective) + `apply_patch`
  + stamped `ProviderBinding` values — four representations of one concept. Each is justified
  (patch semantics, atomic merge, next-reregister cadence), but M1 must keep them coherent.
- **Two-outbox, drain-after-every-call discipline.** A transactional event + provider-command
  outbox on an in-memory kernel with a mandatory drain contract is substantial ceremony for
  milestone M1; the risk is ritual compliance rather than misuse.
- **`PhonebridgeError` pickle/deepcopy args ceremony** (`args=(code, data)`) — correct, but
  it exists to serve a test, not an observed need, on an abstract contract.
- **`RedactedString` + `NewType` layering.** The non-str fail-closed container is justified by
  PII rules; `CallToken`/`MediaToken`/`AssignmentId` NewTypes are runtime identities, so the
  "type safety" is annotation-deep — acceptable, but readers should not over-trust it.
- **Triple validation of `forward_via_line`** (model constructor + `configure_line` + dial-time
  target allowlist) plus a bounded 8-hop transitive walk — consistent with the fail-closed
  posture; the walk itself is not implemented anywhere yet (MAJOR-2).
- **Bounded-everything taxonomy** (`MAX_*` constants, `_Checked*` aliases on every field) —
  mechanical, cheap, and uniformly applied; keep.

